Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Maintainer-chosen 2026-09-01. jtframe derives BOTH the MRA's <name> and its
FILENAME from this description, and the filename is the MiSTer core-list
entry — so this string IS what a user sees when picking the core.

It read "Vampire Savior: The Lord of Vampire (Japan 970519, CPS-2 WIDE v1)",
which is Capcom's title for a build that is NOT stock Vampire Savior, and
sat one letter from the stock control MRA in a sorted list. The shipped
bundles then carried a SHORTER name applied BY HAND during assembly — a
step no tool owned and no gate checked. Naming it here retires that step:
what jtframe emits is now what ships.

The profile version (v1) is deliberately not in the display name; it lives
in the MAME/FBNeo driver entries and in the binaries' own "CPS-2 WIDE v1"
string, which the gates assert.

DESCRIPTION-ONLY: the diff is exactly one line. setname (vsavjw), rbf,
sourcefile, every ROM row and all 31 CRCs are untouched, so nothing
functional moves and no romset re-verification is implied.
---
 doc/mame.xml | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/doc/mame.xml b/doc/mame.xml
index 4b3dc1b23..9dbe3cfc4 100644
--- a/doc/mame.xml
+++ b/doc/mame.xml
@@ -147710,7 +147710,7 @@
 		<driver status="good" emulation="good" savestate="supported"/>
 	</machine>
 <machine name="vsavjw" sourcefile="capcom/cps2w.cpp" cloneof="vsav" romof="vsav">
-		<description>Vampire Savior: The Lord of Vampire (Japan 970519, CPS-2 WIDE v1)</description>
+		<description>Vampire Saved - CPS-2 WIDE (Japan 970519)</description>
 		<year>1997</year>
 		<manufacturer>Capcom</manufacturer>
 		<rom name="vm3j.03d" size="524288" crc="2acc5d6d" sha1="c1f1e1c7ae017564c975a12da740ed983ad3a0ee" region="maincpu" offset="0"/>
